{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EstHJiLMRGe4UiV4tkPhiQ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I see - when stride_idx >= query_stride, the thread computes the hash of an implicitly zero row (since cells are initialized to 0 and never updated). Let me look at the full picture and understand the usage:\n\nBased on my analysis, here's what I found:\n\n## Summary: `calculate_zero_hash` Analysis\n\nI could not find a kernel explicitly named `calculate_zero_hash` in the codebase. However, I've identified the actual kernels and their role in the Merkle tree commitment pipeline. The kernel you're referring to is likely performing the **computation of hashes for padding rows** (rows of all zeros).\n\n### Key Kernels Found:\n\n**1. Primary Row Hashing Kernel: `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel`**\n- **File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu` (lines 16-79)\n- **Also for extension fields**: `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_ext_kernel` (lines 81-149)\n\n**What it computes:**\n- Hashes each row of a matrix using Poseidon2 permutation\n- Simultaneously builds a Merkle tree by compressing `2^log_rows_per_query` strided rows\n- **Padding handling** (line 39): When `stride_idx >= query_stride`, the thread still executes the row hashing logic with an **implicitly zero-initialized row** (cells[i] = Fp(0) at line 36)\n- The Poseidon2 permutation is applied to these zero rows, producing the \"zero hash\" for padding leaves\n\n**Call chain:**\n- CUDA FFI wrapper: `_poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes()` (line 214)\n- Rust bindings: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/merkle_tree.rs` (lines 52-68)\n- Hash scheme dispatch: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/hash_scheme.rs` (line 128)\n- Merkle tree construction: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs` (lines 129-136)\n\n**2. Layer Compression Kernel: `poseidon2_strided_compress_layer_kernel`**\n- **File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu` (lines 153-177)\n- Takes pairs of digests and compresses them using Poseidon2\n- Called for each layer of the tree until the root is reached\n\n### Where Results Are Used:\n\nThe `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes` result is stored in the **leaf layer** of the Merkle tree (`digest_layers[0]`):\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/merkle_tree.rs` (lines 127-142): Allocates output buffer, calls kernel, stores result\n- Used in `MerkleTreeGpu` structure to build subsequent layers through repeated calls to `compress_layer`\n\n### Related CPU Implementation:\n\nThe CPU backend shows the padding pattern explicitly:\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cpu-backend/src/merkle.rs` (lines 360-366): Explicitly computes `zero_row = vec![F::ZERO; width]` and hashes it with `hasher.hash_slice(&zero_row)` for padding leaves\n\n### BN254 Variant:\n\nFor BN254 Poseidon2:\n- **File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/bn254_poseidon2.cu` (lines 183-223)\n- Same structure with `bn254_compressing_row_hashes_kernel`\n- Helper function `bn254_zero_init()` (lines 84-88) initializes Bn254Fr elements to zero\n\nThe 106ms overhead at APC 300 (584 instances) is likely due to computing padding row hashes across many Merkle trees with large powers-of-two padding, where the GPU threads spend cycles hashing implicit zero rows that don't correspond to actual matrix data."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f245fed0-0457-4d6a-9da2-744553774a6f","uuid":"f6fe5664-db42-416e-b3cd-39a9a7aad008","timestamp":"2026-04-15T15:34:33.290Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the `calculate_zero_hash` CUDA kernel and its usage in the Merkle tree commitment pipeline. This kernel accounts for 106ms (584 instances) at APC 300 in the GPU prover.\n\nSearch the codebase (in /home/georg/stark-backend and look at .cu, .cuh, .rs files