xible.\n\nBatching the precompute_logup_combinations and precompute_lambda_combinations calls is another solid opportunity—they're launched hundreds of times per metric, so consolidating them into descriptor-array batches like the MLE approach would save significant time. I need to verify whether these are called once or repeatedly across rounds though.\n\nLooking at the profiling data, it seems precompute_logup_combinations runs once per AIR per segment during the precompute phase that's now overlapped with Round 0. With 623 AIRs across segments and recursion proofs, the 791 instances make sense. But if this is truly overlapped with Round 0, it shouldn't be adding to the wall-clock time—I need to check where those 53ms are actually showing up in the timeline.\n\nThe precompute_lambda_combinations_kernel has 810 instances (close to 791), so it might be called per-round instead of just once during precompute. Let me trace through the code to see if these kernels are being invoked during the MLE round loop or only in the initial precompute phase.\n\nActually, I should stop chasing these details and focus on the highest-impact optimization. Looking at my analysis, the most promising candidates are fusing Round 0 extraction into the evaluation kernels (15-25ms savings, moderate complexity), batching the precompute_lambda_combinations (10ms, simple), or optimizing the GKR K2 build (10-25ms, moderate complexity). Since the GKR fractional sumcheck is the single largest bottleneck at 209ms, I should reconsider whether there's a way to fundamentally accelerate it, maybe through persistent kernels.\n\nA persistent kernel approach for Round 0 could be really effective—instead of launching thousands of individual kernels across the 39 AIRs and reduction passes, I'd pre-allocate a work queue on the GPU and have persistent thread blocks atomically claim and execute work items in a loop. This would eliminate the kernel launch overhead (roughly 5ms from 1000+ launches), remove CPU-