rom CPU-side per-AIR overhead, kernel launch queuing, and SCATTER processing. The pre-allocation work already eliminated the mutex acquisitions and async malloc/free calls, so what's left is the actual CPU work of preparing kernel parameters and the GPU execution itself. I'm wondering if there's still room to optimize the per-AIR CPU overhead, though the batch approach hit L2 cache issues before.\n\nThe real bottleneck might be that for small AIRs, the CPU setup time (50-200μs) is competing with GPU kernel time (100-500μs). If CPU setup dominates at 200μs per AIR, then with 39 AIRs per thread we're looking at roughly 7.8ms of CPU-bound time per thread, which could explain why we're not hitting the theoretical 25ms wall clock.\n\nBut wait—with 312 AIRs per segment distributed round-robin across 8 threads, each thread gets a mix of large and small AIRs. The large ones (height ≥ 2^14) take 5-13ms each, and there are probably around 10 of those per segment. Those large AIRs are what's actually dominating the per-thread execution time, not the small ones.\n\nSo if seg0 is taking 86ms and seg1 is taking 61ms, the difference likely comes down to how many large AIRs ended up in each segment—seg0 probably has more or larger ones. The 86ms also includes all the synchronization overhead and buffer management that I've been trying to account for.\n\nI need to stop second-guessing myself and commit to an optimization strategy.\n\nThe clearest win I'm seeing is fusing the Round 0 extraction kernels directly into the evaluation kernels. Round 0 is already a bottleneck at 179ms total, and right now each AIR spawns 3-5 separate kernels just for zerocheck and logup evaluation plus their extractions. If I fold the extraction logic (the transformation matrix multiply) into the evaluation kernel itself, I eliminate ~1580 kernel launches and their associated synchronization overhead. It's more targeted than trying to batch everything together, and the math is straightforward.\n\nBut wait—I need to reconsider whether this actually saves wall-clock time. The extraction kernels are already pipelined on per-thread CUDA streams, so they're overlapping with evaluation work. And the extraction kernels themselves only cost 26ms total, which was already a win over the old CPU post-processing approach. Fusing them would save maybe 10μs per AIR from eliminating the launch overhead, but that's negligible across the segment. Plus, merging the logic into the evaluation kernel could increase register pressure and actually slow down the evaluation itself.\n\nSo this approach probably won't move the needle much. I need to think about the bottleneck from a completely different angle.\n\nLooking back at what actually delivered big wins: the VPMM page size change gave us 186ms, multi-streaming gave 300ms+, and batch optimizations saved 150-200ms each. The VPMM improvement was the most recent breakthrough because it reduced overhead across all phases. Maybe I can push that further—what if I test different page sizes like 32MB or 24MB instead of the current 16MB? The decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold task regressed when trying to go to 64MB, but there might be a sweet spot in between.\n\nThe thing is, increasing page size to 32MB would cut cuMemCreate calls even further and route 16-32MB allocations through cudaMallocAsync instead of VPMM. But that earlier task showed cudaMallocAsync actually hurts performance for allocations in the 16-64MB range. The difference here is that task changed pool_threshold independently—if I increase page_size to 32MB, smaller allocations bypass VPMM while larger ones use VPMM with bigger pages. The real question is whether the allocation distribution actually has enough traffic in that 16-32MB range to matter.\n\nLooking at the decouple results, routing 16-32MB through cudaMallocAsync caused a +32ms regression at APC 0, which exceeds the 20ms rollback threshold. But what if I use 32MB pages while keeping the threshold at 32MB—matching page size to threshold? That way allocations under 32MB go through cudaMallocAsync while larger ones use VPMM with 32MB pages, potentially avoiding the regression while still reducing cuMemCreate calls.\n\nActually, that would still route the 16-32MB range through cudaMallocAsync, so I'd hit the same regression. VPMM tuning isn't the answer here.\n\nLet me pivot to something different entirely. I'm noticing the MLE rounds use a fold_mle_evals function that halves num_y each round—after 12 rounds, 2^12 becomes 1. What if instead of folding one variable per round, I could fold two variables simultaneously, reducing num_y by a factor of 4 each round?\n\nThat would cut the number of rounds in half and eliminate half the per-round overhead. But the math gets complicated—I'd need four evaluations per fold instead of two, and more importantly, this breaks the sumcheck protocol since the verifier expects exactly one round per variable. Changing the folding structure would fundamentally alter the proof format.\n\nThe constraints are clear though: I can't modify the underlying cryptographic protocol or the verifier itself. So multi-folding is completely off the table.\n\nLet me step back and finalize my best optimization candidates. After working through all the constraints, my top approaches are batching the MLE interpolation and folding steps to tighten per-round overhead, fusing the initial round extraction directly into the evaluation kernels to eliminate redundant work, optimizing the GKR K2 build kernel for better parallelism, and pre-sorting the GKR input evaluation work by kernel type to improve cache locality.\n\nBut honestly, even the most optimistic estimates only get me to around 20ms of improvement, which isn't transformative.\n\nActually, I should look more carefully at where the time is really going in Trace Commit. The numbers show Poseidon2 hashing is taking 231ms across 25 kernel instances, stack columns are only 16ms, and batch NTT is 32ms. Let me dig into what's actually dominating the RS encoding and Merkle tree construction phases.\n\nLooking at the kernel timeline, I'm seeing the NTT variants consume around 112ms total, bit reversal permutation takes 33ms, and the Merkle tree operations—both compress and init—account for 75ms and 71ms respectively. That's roughly 580ms of GPU time spread across a single stream, which translates to about 197ms wall clock. The math here is interesting: if I have 580ms of GPU work on a single stream taking 197ms wall clock, that means the kernels are running sequentially, not in parallel.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the per-segment costs at APC 300. Each segment takes around 90-107ms, and I can trace where that time goes: stack column operations are quick at about 8ms, the RS encoding pipeline with NTT and interpolation runs 20-30ms, Poseidon2 row hashing dominates at 40-60ms per segment, and Merkle tree compression adds another 20-30ms. That breakdown aligns with the observed 90-107ms per segment.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the Poseidon2 row hashing—it's consuming the majority of the per-segment time. I'm wondering if there's room to optimize how the kernel processes the width×height matrix through Poseidon2.\n\nLooking at the 25 total hashing instances across the benchmark run, they're spread across both the app proof segments and recursion proofs, so the timing includes work beyond just the two app segments. For just the app proof alone, I'm estimating maybe 2-4 Poseidon2 calls totaling around 100ms.\n\nI need to stop second-guessing and commit to an optimization direction. The Poseidon2 hashing is clearly the target, and I should focus there rather than continuing to analyze.\n\nLooking at the nsys timeline data, I'm trying to understand where the GPU has idle time between STARK phases. The Round 0 kernels run from 3722ms to 6446ms, and the GKR input evaluation takes 3515ms, so I need to check if there's meaningful overlap or gaps where I could parallelize work across segments. Looking at the timeline more carefully, the GKR input evaluation starts at 3515ms while Round 0 constraint evaluation begins at 3720ms, which suggests they're overlapping rather than strictly sequential. I need to reconcile this with the actual STARK proving order where Round 0 should come before LogUp GKR in the constraint evaluation phase. The `calculate_zero_hash` kernel runs across trace generation and preflight execution, spanning from 719ms to 5135ms, and because these kernels execute on different CUDA streams, the nsight timeline interleaves them with the STARK proving kernels, making it impossible to cleanly separate the phases using just timestamps. Rather than continue dissecting the timeline, I'm going to commit to optimizing the Trace Commit phase by reducing redundant Merkle tree work in the stacked matrix construction.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the data I already have. The realistic optimization targets are either small localized wins of 10-20ms or major structural changes, so I should aim for something in the 30-50ms range that hasn't been explored yet. The RS encoding in stacked_commit—specifically the batch_expand_pad and batch_ntt_small operations—looks like a promising area to investigate.\n\nLooking at the nsight profiling data, I'm seeing the NTT kernels dominate the timeline with batch_ntt_kernel taking 32ms across 17 instances, the CT_NTT variants consuming 82ms and 30ms respectively, bit_rev_permutation_z at 33ms, and mle_interpolate_fused_2d at 10ms. The stacked matrix computation might be doing redundant work across columns with similar heights, which could be where the optimization opportunity lies.\n\nActually, wait—the total NTT time across all proofs is around 187ms, but that's running sequentially on a single stream during Trace Commit. For just the 2 app proof segments, I'm probably looking at closer to 100ms of that. I need to stop digging into the numbers and commit to an optimization strategy.\n\nI'm going to multistream the Trace Commit phase—specifically the RS encoding and Merkle tree construction. Right now everything runs sequentially: stacking columns, RS encoding with NTT and bit reversal, then Poseidon2 hashing rows, and finally compressing the Merkle tree layers. The Merkle tree operations depend on the RS encoding being complete, but I can parallelize some of this work across multiple streams.\n\nActually, the NTT is already a batched operation processing all columns at once, so splitting it across streams won't help. And since the Merkle tree hashes rows, I need all columns encoded before I can hash any row—there's no way to overlap the RS encoding with the Merkle hashing.\n\nLet me think about rearranging the RS encoding to process rows incrementally instead of all at once.\n\nIf I encode rows in small batches and hash them as soon as they're ready, I could pipeline the two stages: encode rows 0 through k, hash those rows, then encode the next batch while hashing continues. The constraint is that the NTT transforms columns from time-domain to frequency-domain, so I can't hash a row until all its columns are transformed. With a row-major approach, I could process all columns for a single row, then immediately hash it, creating a true producer-consumer pipeline.\n\nHowever, the NTT is naturally column-major—it processes one column at a time across all rows. Switching to row-major would require transposing the matrix, which is expensive and probably not worth the overhead.\n\nI'm settling on a different optimization instead: overlapping the MLE fold kernels with the batch evaluation kernels using separate CUDA streams. Right now each MLE round runs the fold kernel, then the eval kernels, then CPU work sequentially on the default stream. By using separate streams, I can let the fold kernel from one round execute concurrently with the eval kernels from the previous round, hiding some of the latency.\n\nActually, wait—the fold for round N+1 depends on the challenge from round N's eval, so they can't overlap across rounds. But within a single round, the fold produces the folded matrices that feed into the eval, so they're still dependent. Let me think about whether there's any parallelism I'm missing here.\n\nThe fold and eval are sequential within each round—the eval can't start until the fold finishes. So there's no overlap opportunity there. I keep circling back to the same conclusion.\n\nLet me focus on what actually matters. After all these optimizations, I've gotten the STARK exclusion trace down from 2455ms to 1106ms at APC 300, which is a 2.22x speedup. The scaling from APC 0 to 300 is only 1.61x though, and I need it above 2x. The real bottleneck now is the GKR fractional sumcheck inner loop, specifically the between-layer operations like K2 build, precompute_m, and multifold—these are taking about 71ms per segment and they're memory-bandwidth-bound kernels on the GKR tree.\n\nActually, looking at the numbers more carefully, the fractional sumcheck itself is already scaling better at APC 300 (105ms) than at APC 0 (117ms), so that's not where the scaling problem is. The issue is in the other components like Round 0 and the MLE Rounds. To hit 2x scaling, I need the APC 300 time down to 892ms, but I'm at 1106ms—that's a 214ms gap I need to close, roughly a 19% improvement. Looking at where those milliseconds are coming from, Round 0 needs to drop from 179ms to 89ms, and the MLE Rounds need similar cuts.\n\nNow I'm wondering if there's a structural approach that could help—what if I could process the two segments in parallel instead of sequentially?\n\nSince each segment is independent and uses 8 multi-stream threads, running both concurrently would mean 16 threads total on the GPU. The RTX 4090 has 128 SMs, so there might be enough capacity. The time would drop from the sum of both segments to just the maximum of the two, bringing APC 300 down to around 581ms if the GPU can handle it. But there are serious constraints: each segment peaks at ~15GB of memory, so two segments would need 30GB total, and the RTX 4090 only has 24GB.\n\nThe bigger question is whether the segments can actually be proven independently. Looking at the SWIRL proof structure, each segment generates its own STARK proof with its own challenges derived from its trace commitment. The segments don't depend on each other's challenges—they're independent proofs that get aggregated later during recursion. So theoretically, they could be generated in parallel.\n\nBut the memory constraint is the real blocker. At APC 300, we'd exceed the GPU's VRAM capacity unless we find a way to reduce the peak memory footprint per segment.\n\nWhat if we stagger the segment proofs so the memory-intensive phases don't collide? If segment 1 starts its trace commitment while segment 0 is in the constraints phase, we could keep the total memory usage around 10GB instead of 30GB—well within the 24GB limit. The key is timing the phases so that segment 0's lighter operations overlap with segment 1's heavier ones. The staggered pipeline would cut total time down to around 630ms—a 43% improvement that brings the scaling to 2.83x. But this requires major structural changes: restructuring the proving loop for concurrent segments, managing GPU memory carefully, coordinating 16 worker threads, and maintaining independent Fiat-Shamir transcripts per segment. It's a significant undertaking, though it wouldn't alter the cryptographic protocol or verifier itself.\n\nA simpler alternative would be to pipeline just seg1's Trace Commit with seg0's later phases, overlapping about 90ms of work. This avoids compute contention since only one segment would be in the heavy Constraints/Openings phase at a time, and the memory footprint stays manageable at around 7GB. The catch is that Trace Commit generates the commitment needed for challenge generation, so I need to think through whether starting it early creates any dependencies that would block the rest of the pipeline.\n\nLooking at the timing, if seg1's Trace Commit runs during seg0's Openings phase, the total time becomes seg0's full duration plus seg1's Constraints and Openings—that's 581 + 433 = 1014ms, saving about 92ms compared to the sequential 1106ms. That gets me to a 1.76x speedup for the APC 0→300 scaling, which is better than the current 1.61x but still short of the 2x target. To hit 2x, I'd need to shave another 214ms, which means I need to explore what other optimizations could combine with this pipelining approach.\n\nWhat if I extend the pipelining strategy to overlap not just Trace Commit but also the early stages of Constraints? If seg1's Trace Commit starts when seg0's Constraints begins, both can run in parallel on different streams—seg0 Constraints finishes at 477ms while seg1 Trace Commit completes at 197ms, creating more overlap and potentially getting closer to the 892ms target needed for true 2x performance. - Seg1 Constraints start: max(197, 477) = 477ms (after both TC done and seg0 Constraints done)\n\nWait, but seg1's Constraints depends on:\n1. Seg1's Trace Commit output (commitment, challenges)\n2. Seg1's trace data (already in GPU memory from trace gen)\n\nIt does NOT depend on seg0's Constraints. So seg1's Constraints could start as soon as seg1's TC is done (197ms), even while seg0's Constraints are still running (107-477ms).\n\nThe overlap would be:\n```\nSeg0: [TC 0-107] [Constraints 107-477] [Openings 477-581]\nSeg1:   [TC 107-197] [Constraints 197-533] [Openings 533-630]\n```\n\nSo the timeline becomes: Seg0 TC runs alone until 107ms, then Seg0 Constraints and Seg1 TC overlap from 107-197ms. From 197-477ms, both segments are computing constraints simultaneously, which means they're competing for the same GPU resources—16 threads total fighting over 128 SMs. After that, Seg0 moves to Openings while Seg1 finishes Constraints, and they continue overlapping through the Openings phases until completion at 630ms. The memory pressure during the 197-477ms window is particularly tight, with Seg0's evaluation buffers and Seg1's constraint data both occupying GPU memory. GB)\n- Seg1 Constraints: evaluation buffers (~5-8GB)  \n- Total: ~10-16GB, potentially OK on 24GB GPU\n\nThe compute competition would slow each segment. If it's 50% slowdown, then:\n- Seg0 Constraints: 370ms → 555ms (1.5x)\n- Seg1 Constraints: 336ms → 504ms (1.5x)\n\nTimeline with slowdown:\n```\nSeg0: [TC 0-107] [Constraints 107-662] [Openings 662-765]\nSeg1:   [TC 107-197] [Constraints 197-701] [Openings 701-798]\n```\n\nTotal: 798ms. Still much better than 1106ms! Even with 50% slowdown from competition.\n\nWhether 50% slowdown is realistic depends on memory bandwidth constraints—with 128 SMs and multi-streaming, compute might not be the bottleneck, but if memory bandwidth is saturated, two segments would each see roughly that degradation. This is getting speculative though, so let me focus on what I can actually implement.\n\nI'm going to propose overlapping segment proving by pipelining seg1's proving with seg0's later phases. It's a significant architectural change, but it's the highest-impact optimization left, doesn't alter the protocol or verifier, and the GPU has the capacity with its 128 SMs and 24GB VRAM. The approach would spawn seg1's proving on background threads using separate CUDA streams for each segment.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the memory actually fits. At APC 300, each segment peaks around 15-16GB, so two segments would need roughly 31-32GB—that exceeds the 24GB limit. But looking more carefully at the actual constraint phase memory usage, seg1 only needs evaluation buffers of 5-8GB while seg0 is in its later phases, so the overlap might actually stay within budget at 10-16GB total. 's a problem. But the peak only occurs briefly during specific phases. If the phases are staggered to avoid peak overlap, total memory might stay within 24GB.\n\nPeak memory occurs during:\n- Trace Commit: RS codeword matrix (~192MB) + Merkle tree (~500MB) = ~700MB\n- Constraints: pre-allocated buffers + evaluation buffers + batch arrays\n- Openings: smaller memory footprint\n\nThe \"in pool\" metric of 14.2GB is the VPMM pool reservation, not actual usage. The physical pages in use might be much less.\n\nLooking at the actual GPU memory metrics, each segment only uses about 1.5-1.6GB during proving, so two segments would need roughly 3.2GB—well within the 24GB limit. The 14.2GB pool represents reserved virtual address space shared across all allocations, and since both segments would draw from the same global VPMM pool, I need to ensure there are enough free pages available when both are actively allocating simultaneously. With each segment's working set around 1.5GB, the pool should have sufficient headroom.\n\nMemory isn't the constraint here. The real bottleneck is GPU compute contention—both segments competing for the same GPU cores simultaneously.\n\nThe segments themselves are completely independent: each has its own trace commits, constraint evaluations, openings, and Fiat-Shamir transcript. The only shared resources are the read-only proving key and the GPU itself. So the implementation would involve spawning each segment's proving process on separate threads or async tasks.\n\nLooking at how segments are currently proved sequentially in a loop, I'd refactor that to spawn background threads for each segment and then collect the results once they all complete. The good news is the VPMM and CUDA allocations are already thread-safe, so the main work is just handling the thread spawning and result aggregation.\n\nBut I need to verify a few things first—whether the proving key has any mutable state, if segments can truly be proved independently, and how the final proof aggregates the individual segment proofs in the recursion step.\n\nLooking at the code structure, each segment's proof is a standalone STARK proof that the verifier checks independently, and the recursion step chains them together without requiring sequential generation. So parallel proving is feasible as long as each segment has its own transcript, the proving key stays read-only, and GPU resources don't conflict.\n\nThe real issue is figuring out where the segment proving loop lives—if it's in OpenVM rather than stark-backend, I can't modify it directly, but I could provide APIs in stark-backend that enable parallelization. Let me check the metrics to see where the bottleneck actually is.\n\nThe `prove_segment_time_ms` is reported per segment, which means the loop iterating over segments is likely in the OpenVM SDK, while stark-backend just provides the `prove()` function for individual segments. That means parallelizing segments would require changes outside stark-backend, which violates the constraint.\n\nSo I need to focus on optimizations within a single segment's proving instead. Looking at the timing breakdown, the top candidates are reducing the per-AIR overhead in Round 0 (around 90ms), reducing per-round overhead in the MLE rounds (around 82ms), or improving the GKR inner loop where fractional sumcheck takes about 105ms.\n\nFor the Round 0 optimization, I'm considering a more aggressive pipelining approach where CPU setup is separated from GPU execution—pre-building all work items in a single-threaded phase before distributing them to the 8 GPU threads. But looking at the actual code structure, Phase 1 already does the work item building and Phase 2 handles the multi-threaded execution, so this separation already exists.\n\nThe real bottleneck in Phase 2 is the per-AIR CPU overhead that still happens during kernel launches—things like preparing CUDA parameters. If I pre-computed all kernel launch parameters in Phase 1, Phase 2 could just fire off kernels with no CPU work in between. That would save roughly 6ms per thread, or about 12ms total across the segment. But that's not enough of a gain to justify the effort.\n\nSo I'm shifting focus: what if I reduced the number of kernel launches per AIR instead?\n\nCurrently each AIR needs 4-6 kernel launches depending on whether it's single-block or multi-block—zerocheck eval, zerocheck extraction, logup eval, logup extraction, plus optional reduction kernels. For 312 AIRs per segment, that's around 1248 launches total. The launch overhead itself is only about 6ms per segment, so that's not the main problem either.\n\nLooking at the actual GPU profiling data, the evaluation and extraction kernels are taking roughly 700ms per proof across both segments, which works out to about 87ms per thread.\n\nThe real issue is that GPU compute time doesn't scale well with more AIRs because each kernel invocation has fixed overhead on the GPU side—loading descriptor parameters, computing addresses, and other setup work. Even though the total number of cells decreases, the number of kernel launches increases, and each one carries that fixed cost. The only real solution would be batching multiple small AIRs into single kernel launches, but that approach has hit repeated bugs and blockers. Let me trace back through what went wrong with those attempts and whether there's a viable variant.\n\nLooking at the history of batching attempts, the CUDA kernels themselves from the April 13 work are actually committed and working. The problem seems to be in how the Rust orchestration layer is using them—either through optimizer interference causing illegal memory access errors, or downstream issues like GKR running out of memory when the batched path is enabled.\n\nThe VPMM page size increase to 16MB should help by routing medium allocations through cudaMallocAsync instead, reducing memory fragmentation. And PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS is already applied to cut down shared memory usage. So the real question now is whether enabling the batch Round 0 path would actually work with these recent fixes in place. I need to reconsider the timeline here—the VPMM page size improvement actually happened before the batch descriptors task, so it was already in effect when that OOM occurred. Looking at the batch task report more carefully, the GPU memory issue isn't specific to the batch code changes; it's a general problem where any CUDA rebuild increases the cubin/fatbin size enough to trigger OOM on the tight GPU memory, and the current codebase has already been rebuilt.\n\nBut here's the thing—APC 300 just ran successfully at 1106ms after the 17:29 rebuild with PRUNE_SHMEM enabled, which means the OOM is actually gone now. The batch Round 0 CUDA kernels might be viable again since the blocker appears to have been cleared by some combination of the shared memory pruning, VPMM improvements, and code removals that reduced the binary size. The infrastructure is already in the codebase and just needs to be re-enabled, which could unlock meaningful performance gains. Let me check what's currently committed and what activation code needs to be restored. I need to trace through the git history to see if those earlier batch CUDA kernel commits from April 13 were also reverted or if they're still present in the codebase.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19501,"cache_read_input_tokens":121085,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19501},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f245fed0-0457-4d6a-9da2-744553774a6f","uuid":"c7e892f3-d5da-463a-b569-46984e0be2e8"}
