{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N3wKMVgYNAPTkaGrVveFam","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Re-enable Batch Round 0 Descriptor-Array Kernels for Small AIRs\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce Round 0 wall-clock time at APC 300 by batching the ~250 small coset-parallel AIRs per segment into 2-4 descriptor-array kernel launches (zerocheck + logup × num_coset_groups) instead of ~1000 individual launches. The current per-AIR kernel launch overhead (launch queuing + extraction + stream serialization) dominates Round 0 at APC 300 where most AIRs are tiny (height ≤ 256). Expected savings: 25-40ms on Round 0, improving STARK excl trace from 1106ms to ~1066-1081ms.\n6\t\n7\tThis optimization was previously implemented 3 times (commits `0c699f47`, `bcb1c0d2`, `0d1bb2f9`) and reverted each time due to GPU OOM at APC 100/300. The OOM has since been resolved by the VPMM page size increase to 16 MiB (`4863c316`) and PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS (`d491ff87`), as confirmed by the current successful APC 300 benchmark (1106ms).\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t### Phase 1 (single-threaded, `mod.rs:1022-1198`)\n12\tBuilds `Vec<Round0AirWorkItem>` for all AIRs. Each work item contains:\n13\t- Air proving key references, trace data pointers\n14\t- Pre-computed buffer sizes (`zc_intermed_cap`, `logup_intermed_cap`)\n15\t- `eq_3bs` (logup interaction data)\n16\t- Per-AIR `Round0ExtractTables` (pre-computed transformation matrices for GPU extraction)\n17\t\n18\tWork items are sorted by descending height. Buffer sizes at the 95th percentile are computed for per-thread pre-allocation.\n19\t\n20\t### Phase 1.5 (overlapped background thread, `mod.rs:1341-1393`)\n21\tUploads `d_eq_3b_per_trace` and precomputes `logup_combinations` on a background thread, concurrent with Phase 2.\n22\t\n23\t### Phase 2 (multi-threaded, `mod.rs:1319-1413`)\n24\t8 OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams (cudaStreamPerThread). Round-robin assignment of sorted work items. Each thread calls `process_air_round0()` per AIR, which:\n25\t1. Calls `evaluate_round0_zerocheck_gpu()` → zerocheck eval kernel + reduction\n26\t2. Calls `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu()` → logup eval kernel + reduction\n27\t3. Calls `_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly()` → GPU extraction kernel writing to `d_batch_array`\n28\t4. Calls `_round0_extract_logup_polys()` → GPU extraction kernel writing to `d_batch_array`\n29\t\n30\tAt APC 300: 312 AIRs/segment × 4-6 kernel launches each = ~1500 launches/segment. Wall clock: ~90ms/segment.\n31\t\n32\t### Why it's slow at APC 300\n33\tPer-AIR overhead: Each AIR requires CPU-side `SymbolicConstraints::from()` (~0.1ms), kernel launch parameters setup, kernel launch queuing (~5μs), stream serialization. For ~250 small AIRs with GPU kernel time <100μs each, the per-AIR overhead exceeds the compute time.\n34\t\n35\t## Changes\n36\t\n37\t### Change 1: CUDA Batched Kernels\n38\t\n39\t**Files:**\n40\t- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu`\n41\t- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu`\n42\t\n43\tAdd batched kernel functions that process multiple AIRs in a single launch using descriptor arrays. Replicate the design from reverted commit `0d1bb2f9`:\n44\t\n45\t- **`batched_zerocheck_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`**: Each thread block is assigned to one AIR via `Round0BlockCtx` (mapping block → AIR descriptor). Each `Round0ZcCtx` descriptor contains all parameters needed for one AIR's zerocheck evaluation (pointers to main/preprocessed traces, constraint config, eq_xis, sels, output pointer). Only instantiate `GLOBAL=true, NEEDS_SHMEM=false` template variant (PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS is active).\n46\t\n47\t- **`batched_logup_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`**: Same pattern with `Round0LogupCtx` descriptors containing interaction weights, rules, eq_3b data, and output pointers.\n48\t\n49\t- **`batched_final_reduce_block_sums`**: Shared reduction kernel (already exists from batch MLE work, reuse the pattern).\n50\t\n51\tThe block-to-AIR mapping (`Round0BlockCtx`) is a flat array where each entry stores `(air_idx, local_block_idx_x)`. Binary search in the kernel maps `blockIdx.x` → descriptor. This is the same pattern used successfully in `batch_mle.cu`.\n52\t\n53\t### Change 2: FFI Bindings\n54\t\n55\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n56\t\n57\tAdd `#[repr(C)]` Rust structs mirroring the CUDA descriptors:\n58\t- `Round0BlockCtx { air_idx: u32, local_block_idx_x: u32 }`\n59\t- `Round0ZcCtx` with all fields matching the CUDA struct (main trace ptr, preprocessed ptr, buffer_size, num_x, skip_domain, eq_xis ptr, sels ptr, constraint_degree, output offset, etc.)\n60\t- `Round0LogupCtx` with interaction evaluation fields\n61\t\n62\tAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers:\n63\t- `batched_zerocheck_r0_eval_constraints(block_ctxs, descs, intermediates, temp_sums, ...)`\n64\t- `batched_logup_r0_eval_interactions(block_ctxs, descs, intermediates, temp_sums, ...)`\n65\t\n66\t### Change 3: Batch Orchestration Module\n67\t\n68\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0_batched.rs` (new file)\n69\t\n70\tAll functions marked `#[inline(never)]` to prevent Rust optimizer interference (the root cause of the original `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` — confirmed by the `batch-round0-caller-routing` task).\n71\t\n72\t**`identify_batchable_airs(work_items, l_skip) -> Vec<bool>`**\n73\t- Mark AIRs as batchable if: (a) coset-parallel variant (`num_x * skip_domain < COSET_PARALLEL_THRESHOLD`), (b) intermediates fit budget (`< MAX_INTERMEDIATES_PER_AIR = 16M Fp`), (c) AIR has zerocheck constraints or logup interactions\n74\t- Return all-false if fewer than `MIN_BATCHABLE_AIRS = 50` qualify (skip batching at APC 0)\n75\t\n76\t**`batch_round0_small_airs(work_items, batch_mask, ...) -> Result<()>`**\n77\t1. Group batchable AIRs by `num_cosets` (constraint_degree determines the number of NTT cosets; AIRs with different num_cosets can't share a kernel launch)\n78\t2. For each group:\n79\t   a. Allocate a shared intermediates arena (`DeviceBuffer`) sized to fit all AIRs in the group\n80\t   b. Build `Round0ZcCtx`/`Round0LogupCtx` descriptor arrays and `Round0BlockCtx` block mapping\n81\t   c. Upload descriptors to GPU\n82\t   d. Launch batched zerocheck eval + reduction\n83\t   e. Launch batched logup eval + reduction\n84\t   f. For each batched AIR: call existing per-AIR extraction kernels (`_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly`, `_round0_extract_logup_polys`) to write results into `d_batch_array`\n85\t\n86\t**Key design choice: Reuse existing extraction kernels.** The previous attempt tried to also batch extraction, but per-AIR extraction is simpler and already fast (~50μs each). The main savings come from batching the evaluation kernels, not extraction.\n87\t\n88\t**Memory budget:** The shared intermediates arena size = sum of per-AIR intermediates. With `MAX_INTERMEDIATES_PER_AIR = 16M Fp` and ~250 AIRs, worst case is ~250 × 16M × 4B = 16GB. In practice, most small AIRs need <64KB intermediates, so the arena is ~10-50MB total. This fits comfortably in the 72MB L2 cache.\n89\t\n90\t**Null intermediates guard:** AIRs with `zc_intermed_cap == 0` (no zerocheck constraints) get a valid non-null pointer offset into the arena (pointing to a harmless region). This prevents the `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` that occurred in the original implementation when the kernel dereferenced a null intermediates pointer.\n91\t\n92\t### Change 4: Integration into Phase 2\n93\t\n94\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n95\t\n96\tAfter Phase 1 (work items built, `d_batch_array` allocated) and before the `thread::scope` block:\n97\t\n98\t```rust\n99\t// Batch small AIRs (between Phase 1 and Phase 2)\n100\tlet batch_mask = round0_batched::identify_batchable_airs(&work_items, l_skip);\n101\tif batch_mask.iter().any(|&b| b) {\n102\t    round0_batched::batch_round0_small_airs(\n103\t        &work_items, &batch_mask, &extract_tables,\n104\t        d_batch_array.as_mut_ptr(), /* ... other params ... */\n105\t    )?;\n106\t    current_stream_sync()?;\n107\t}\n108\t```\n109\t\n110\tIn Phase 2, filter out batched AIRs:\n111\t```rust\n112\t// In the thread worker loop:\n113\tfor &item_idx in &thread_items[thread_id] {\n114\t    if batch_mask[item_idx] { continue; } // Already processed by batch path\n115\t    process_air_round0(&work_items[item_idx], ...)?;\n116\t}\n117\t```\n118\t\n119\t### Change 5: Make work item types accessible\n120\t\n121\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n122\t\n123\tChange `Round0AirWorkItem` and `Round0ExtractTables` visibility from `pub(super)` to `pub(crate)` (or keep `pub(super)` and put `round0_batched.rs` in the same module). The batch module needs read access to work item fields.\n124\t\n125\t## Invariants\n126\t\n127\t1. **Correctness**: Batched evaluation must produce the same `d_batch_array` content as per-AIR evaluation. The batched kernel computes the same coset-parallel NTT + constraint evaluation as the per-AIR kernel, just for multiple AIRs in one launch. Extraction uses the identical per-AIR kernels.\n128\t\n129\t2. **APC 0 regression guard**: `MIN_BATCHABLE_AIRS = 50` ensures the batch path is disabled at APC 0 (which has ~20 AIRs per segment). The per-AIR path remains unchanged for all non-batched AIRs.\n130\t\n131\t3. **Memory safety**: The shared intermediates arena allocates enough capacity for all batched AIRs. Each AIR's descriptor points to its own offset within the arena. The null-intermediates guard ensures no AIR has a null pointer in the descriptor, even if it doesn't use intermediates.\n132\t\n133\t4. **Stream ordering**: The batch path runs on the default stream before `thread::scope`. `current_stream_sync()` after the batch ensures all batched results are visible to Phase 2 worker threads. Phase 2 workers only process non-batched AIRs.\n134\t\n135\t5. **Logup rule reconstruction**: Each batched AIR needs its logup DAG/rules for evaluation. The batch orchestration calls `build_logup_rules_for_air()` per batchable AIR. This per-AIR CPU work was measured at ~0.1ms/AIR; for ~250 AIRs in a single thread, this is ~25ms — acceptable since it runs on the default stream before Phase 2 starts (and previously this work happened inside Phase 2 anyway, distributed across 8 threads at ~3ms/thread).\n136\t\n137\t6. **Extraction correctness**: The per-AIR extraction kernels (`_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly`, `_round0_extract_logup_polys`) write to `d_batch_array` at offsets determined by `trace_idx`. The batch path passes the correct `trace_idx` for each AIR, so extraction writes go to the same locations as the per-AIR path.\n138\t\n139\t## Measurement Plan\n140\t\n141\t1. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`\n142\t2. APC 0 regression check:\n143\t   ```\n144\t   RUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics autoopt-results/after_apc000.json --recursion\n145\t   ```\n146\t   Verify Round 0 ≤ 180ms and STARK excl trace ≤ 1810ms (within noise of current 1784ms).\n147\t3. APC 300 measurement:\n148\t   ```\n149\t   RUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics autoopt-results/after_apc300.json --recursion\n150\t   ```\n151\t   Expect Round 0 ~140-155ms (down from 179ms) and STARK excl trace ~1066-1081ms.\n152\t4. APC 100 measurement for completeness.\n153\t5. Analyze with `python3 autoopt/spec.py <metrics> <name>`.\n154\t\n155\t## Rollback Criteria\n156\t\n157\t- **Hard revert**: APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by >20ms vs current 1784ms\n158\t- **Hard revert**: Any APC configuration produces an incorrect proof (verification failure)\n159\t- **Hard revert**: GPU OOM or `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` at any APC configuration\n160\t- **Soft revert**: APC 300 Round 0 improvement is <10ms (below noise threshold)\n161\t- **Soft revert**: APC 300 STARK excl trace improvement is <20ms (below noise threshold)\n162\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f245fed0-0457-4d6a-9da2-744553774a6f","uuid":"319bb2c1-1439-4cfb-8948-0f7fe4664158","timestamp":"2026-04-15T16:07:36.499Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-1800-reenable-batch-round0-small-airs/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Re-enable Batch Round 0 Descriptor-Array Kernels for Small AIRs\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce Round 0 wall-clock time at APC 300 by batching the ~250 small coset-parallel AIRs per segment into 2-4 descriptor-array kernel launches (zerocheck + logup × num_coset_groups) instead of ~1000 individual launches. The current per-AIR kernel launch overhead (launch queuing + extraction + stream serialization) dominates Round 0 at APC 300 where most AIRs are tiny (height ≤ 256). Expected savings: 25-40ms on Round 0, improving STARK excl trace from 1106ms to ~1066-1081ms.\n\nThis optimization was previously implemented 3 times (commits `0c699f47`, `bcb1c0d2`, `0d1bb2f9`) and reverted each time due to GPU OOM at APC 100/300. The OOM has since been resolved by the VPMM page size increase to 16 MiB (`4863c316`) and PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS (`d491ff87`), as confirmed by the current successful APC 300 benchmark (1106ms).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Phase 1 (single-threaded, `mod.rs:1022-1198`)\nBuilds `Vec<Round0AirWorkItem>` for all AIRs. Each work item contains:\n- Air proving key references, trace data pointers\n- Pre-computed buffer sizes (`zc_intermed_cap`, `logup_intermed_cap`)\n- `eq_3bs` (logup interaction data)\n- Per-AIR `Round0ExtractTables` (pre-computed transformation matrices for GPU extraction)\n\nWork items are sorted by descending height. Buffer sizes at the 95th percentile are computed for per-thread pre-allocation.\n\n### Phase 1.5 (overlapped background thread, `mod.rs:1341-1393`)\nUploads `d_eq_3b_per_trace` and precomputes `logup_combinations` on a background thread, concurrent with Phase 2.\n\n### Phase 2 (multi-threaded, `mod.rs:1319-1413`)\n8 OS threads with per-thread CUDA streams (cudaStreamPerThread). Round-robin assignment of sorted work items. Each thread calls `process_air_round0()` per AIR, which:\n1. Calls `evaluate_round0_zerocheck_gpu()` → zerocheck eval kernel + reduction\n2. Calls `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu()` → logup eval kernel + reduction\n3. Calls `_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly()` → GPU extraction kernel writing to `d_batch_array`\n4. Calls `_round0_extract_logup_polys()` → GPU extraction kernel writing to `d_batch_array`\n\nAt APC 300: 312 AIRs/segment × 4-6 kernel launches each = ~1500 launches/segment. Wall clock: ~90ms/segment.\n\n### Why it's slow at APC 300\nPer-AIR overhead: Each AIR requires CPU-side `SymbolicConstraints::from()` (~0.1ms), kernel launch parameters setup, kernel launch queuing (~5μs), stream serialization. For ~250 small AIRs with GPU kernel time <100μs each, the per-AIR overhead exceeds the compute time.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: CUDA Batched Kernels\n\n**Files:**\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu`\n\nAdd batched kernel functions that process multiple AIRs in a single launch using descriptor arrays. Replicate the design from reverted commit `0d1bb2f9`:\n\n- **`batched_zerocheck_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`**: Each thread block is assigned to one AIR via `Round0BlockCtx` (mapping block → AIR descriptor). Each `Round0ZcCtx` descriptor contains all parameters needed for one AIR's zerocheck evaluation (pointers to main/preprocessed traces, constraint config, eq_xis, sels, output pointer). Only instantiate `GLOBAL=true, NEEDS_SHMEM=false` template variant (PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS is active).\n\n- **`batched_logup_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`**: Same pattern with `Round0LogupCtx` descriptors containing interaction weights, rules, eq_3b data, and output pointers.\n\n- **`batched_final_reduce_block_sums`**: Shared reduction kernel (already exists from batch MLE work, reuse the pattern).\n\nThe block-to-AIR mapping (`Round0BlockCtx`) is a flat array where each entry stores `(air_idx, local_block_idx_x)`. Binary search in the kernel maps `blockIdx.x` → descriptor. This is the same pattern used successfully in `batch_mle.cu`.\n\n### Change 2: FFI Bindings\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd `#[repr(C)]` Rust structs mirroring the CUDA descriptors:\n- `Round0BlockCtx { air_idx: u32, local_block_idx_x: u32 }`\n- `Round0ZcCtx` with all fields matching the CUDA struct (main trace ptr, preprocessed ptr, buffer_size, num_x, skip_domain, eq_xis ptr, sels ptr, constraint_degree, output offset, etc.)\n- `Round0LogupCtx` with interaction evaluation fields\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe Rust wrappers:\n- `batched_zerocheck_r0_eval_constraints(block_ctxs, descs, intermediates, temp_sums, ...)`\n- `batched_logup_r0_eval_interactions(block_ctxs, descs, intermediates, temp_sums, ...)`\n\n### Change 3: Batch Orchestration Module\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0_batched.rs` (new file)\n\nAll functions marked `#[inline(never)]` to prevent Rust optimizer interference (the root cause of the original `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` — confirmed by the `batch-round0-caller-routing` task).\n\n**`identify_batchable_airs(work_items, l_skip) -> Vec<bool>`**\n- Mark AIRs as batchable if: (a) coset-parallel variant (`num_x * skip_domain < COSET_PARALLEL_THRESHOLD`), (b) intermediates fit budget (`< MAX_INTERMEDIATES_PER_AIR = 16M Fp`), (c) AIR has zerocheck constraints or logup interactions\n- Return all-false if fewer than `MIN_BATCHABLE_AIRS = 50` qualify (skip batching at APC 0)\n\n**`batch_round0_small_airs(work_items, batch_mask, ...) -> Result<()>`**\n1. Group batchable AIRs by `num_cosets` (constraint_degree determines the number of NTT cosets; AIRs with different num_cosets can't share a kernel launch)\n2. For each group:\n   a. Allocate a shared intermediates arena (`DeviceBuffer`) sized to fit all AIRs in the group\n   b. Build `Round0ZcCtx`/`Round0LogupCtx` descriptor arrays and `Round0BlockCtx` block mapping\n   c. Upload descriptors to GPU\n   d. Launch batched zerocheck eval + reduction\n   e. Launch batched logup eval + reduction\n   f. For each batched AIR: call existing per-AIR extraction kernels (`_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly`, `_round0_extract_logup_polys`) to write results into `d_batch_array`\n\n**Key design choice: Reuse existing extraction kernels.** The previous attempt tried to also batch extraction, but per-AIR extraction is simpler and already fast (~50μs each). The main savings come from batching the evaluation kernels, not extraction.\n\n**Memory budget:** The shared intermediates arena size = sum of per-AIR intermediates. With `MAX_INTERMEDIATES_PER_AIR = 16M Fp` and ~250 AIRs, worst case is ~250 × 16M × 4B = 16GB. In practice, most small AIRs need <64KB intermediates, so the arena is ~10-50MB total. This fits comfortably in the 72MB L2 cache.\n\n**Null intermediates guard:** AIRs with `zc_intermed_cap == 0` (no zerocheck constraints) get a valid non-null pointer offset into the arena (pointing to a harmless region). This prevents the `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` that occurred in the original implementation when the kernel dereferenced a null intermediates pointer.\n\n### Change 4: Integration into Phase 2\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nAfter Phase 1 (work items built, `d_batch_array` allocated) and before the `thread::scope` block:\n\n```rust\n// Batch small AIRs (between Phase 1 and Phase 2)\nlet batch_mask = round0_batched::identify_batchable_airs(&work_items, l_skip);\nif batch_mask.iter().any(|&b| b) {\n    round0_batched::batch_round0_small_airs(\n        &work_items, &batch_mask, &extract_tables,\n        d_batch_array.as_mut_ptr(), /* ... other params ... */\n    )?;\n    current_stream_sync()?;\n}\n```\n\nIn Phase 2, filter out batched AIRs:\n```rust\n// In the thread worker loop:\nfor &item_idx in &thread_items[thread_id] {\n    if batch_mask[item_idx] { continue; } // Already processed by batch path\n    process_air_round0(&work_items[item_idx], ...)?;\n}\n```\n\n### Change 5: Make work item types accessible\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nChange `Round0AirWorkItem` and `Round0ExtractTables` visibility from `pub(super)` to `pub(crate)` (or keep `pub(super)` and put `round0_batched.rs` in the same module). The batch module needs read access to work item fields.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Batched evaluation must produce the same `d_batch_array` content as per-AIR evaluation. The batched kernel computes the same coset-parallel NTT + constraint evaluation as the per-AIR kernel, just for multiple AIRs in one launch. Extraction uses the identical per-AIR kernels.\n\n2. **APC 0 regression guard**: `MIN_BATCHABLE_AIRS = 50` ensures the batch path is disabled at APC 0 (which has ~20 AIRs per segment). The per-AIR path remains unchanged for all non-batched AIRs.\n\n3. **Memory safety**: The shared intermediates arena allocates enough capacity for all batched AIRs. Each AIR's descriptor points to its own offset within the arena. The null-intermediates guard ensures no AIR has a null pointer in the descriptor, even if it doesn't use intermediates.\n\n4. **Stream ordering**: The batch path runs on the default stream before `thread::scope`. `current_stream_sync()` after the batch ensures all batched results are visible to Phase 2 worker threads. Phase 2 workers only process non-batched AIRs.\n\n5. **Logup rule reconstruction**: Each batched AIR needs its logup DAG/rules for evaluation. The batch orchestration calls `build_logup_rules_for_air()` per batchable AIR. This per-AIR CPU work was measured at ~0.1ms/AIR; for ~250 AIRs in a single thread, this is ~25ms — acceptable since it runs on the default stream before Phase 2 starts (and previously this work happened inside Phase 2 anyway, distributed across 8 threads at ~3ms/thread).\n\n6. **Extraction correctness**: The per-AIR extraction kernels (`_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly`, `_round0_extract_logup_polys`) write to `d_batch_array` at offsets determined by `trace_idx`. The batch path passes the correct `trace_idx` for each AIR, so extraction writes go to the same locations as the per-AIR path.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`\n2. APC 0 regression check:\n   ```\n   RUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics autoopt-results/after_apc000.json --recursion\n   ```\n   Verify Round 0 ≤ 180ms and STARK excl trace ≤ 1810ms (within noise of current 1784ms).\n3. APC 300 measurement:\n   ```\n   RUST_LOG=info $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics autoopt-results/after_apc300.json --recursion\n   ```\n   Expect Round 0 ~140-155ms (down from 179ms) and STARK excl trace ~1066-1081ms.\n4. APC 100 measurement for completeness.\n5. Analyze with `python3 autoopt/spec.py <metrics> <name>`.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- **Hard revert**: APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses by >20ms vs current 1784ms\n- **Hard revert**: Any APC configuration produces an incorrect proof (verification failure)\n- **Hard revert**: GPU OOM or `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` at any APC configuration\n- **Soft revert**: APC 300 Round 0 improvement is <10ms (below noise threshold)\n- **Soft revert**: APC 300 STARK excl trace improvement is <20ms (below noise threshold)\n","numLines":162,"startLine":1,"totalLines":162}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RyjbSHVPXWS5KFxokZDktz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WtDjZJyDEuXkva5qoQfith","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-1800-reenable-batch-round0-small-airs/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Re-enable Batch Round 0 Descriptor-Array Kernels for Small AIRs\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce Round 0 wall-clock time at APC 300 by batching the ~250 small coset-parallel AIRs per segment. The batch path runs eval+extraction on the default CUDA stream before the multi-threaded Phase 2, which then only processes the remaining ~60 large AIRs. This eliminates ~500 eval kernel launches per segment and moves ~250 AIRs' CPU overhead (SymbolicConstraints::from, rule building) out of the multi-threaded critical path.\n\nCurrent: Round 0 = 179ms (90ms/seg). Expected after: Round 0 = 120-145ms (60-73ms/seg). Net savings: 35-60ms on STARK excl trace (1106ms → 1046-1071ms).\n\nPrevious implementations (commits `0c699f47`, `bcb1c0d2`, `0d1bb2f9`) were reverted due to GPU OOM at APC 100/300. The OOM has since been resolved (VPMM 16 MiB page size + PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS), confirmed by the current APC 300 run at 1106ms.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\n### Phase 1 (single-threaded, lines 1022-1198)\nBuilds `Vec<Round0AirWorkItem>` for all AIRs. Each work item has: proving key refs, trace pointers, pre-computed buffer sizes, eq_3bs, `Round0ExtractTables`. Items sorted by descending height. Percentile buffer sizes computed for pre-allocation.\n\n### Phase 1.5 (overlapped background, lines 1341-1393)\nBackground thread uploads `d_eq_3b_per_trace` and precomputes `logup_combinations`, concurrent with Phase 2.\n\n### Phase 2 (multi-threaded, lines 1319-1413)\n8 OS threads, per-thread CUDA streams, round-robin assignment. Per AIR: `process_air_round0()` does:\n1. `evaluate_round0_zerocheck_gpu()` → zerocheck eval + reduction\n2. `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu()` → calls `SymbolicConstraints::from()`, builds DAG, encodes rules, H2D uploads, logup eval + reduction\n3. `_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly()` → GPU extraction to `d_batch_array`\n4. `_round0_extract_logup_polys()` → GPU extraction to `d_batch_array`\n\nAt APC 300: ~312 AIRs/seg, ~4 kernel launches/AIR = ~1248 eval+extraction launches/seg. Wall clock: ~90ms/seg.\n\n### Why it's slow at APC 300\nPhase 2 processes ~312 AIRs across 8 threads (~39/thread). For the ~250 small AIRs (height ≤ 256), each one has:\n- CPU cost: `SymbolicConstraints::from()` (~0.1ms) + DAG build + rule encode in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu()` (~0.1ms)\n- GPU eval time: ~50-200μs (tiny, GPU-underutilized)\n- 4 kernel launches with ~10μs overhead each\n\nThe per-AIR CPU work (~0.2ms) dominates over GPU compute for small AIRs. 250 small AIRs × 0.2ms / 8 threads ≈ 6ms of serialized CPU overhead per thread. The GPU eval of these small AIRs takes ~4ms/thread but is interleaved with large AIRs.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 0: Diagnose the previous GPU hang\n\n**Before any code changes**, run the reverted batch Round 0 kernels from commit `0d1bb2f9` under `compute-sanitizer --tool memcheck` to identify whether the 12-minute hang at APC 300 (reported in the 1600 report) was caused by an out-of-bounds memory access, infinite loop, or other bug. Steps:\n1. Cherry-pick `0d1bb2f9` onto a throwaway branch\n2. Build with debug symbols\n3. Run: `compute-sanitizer --tool memcheck -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion`\n4. If memcheck identifies an OOB access: fix the specific bug in the new implementation\n5. If memcheck shows no errors: the hang was likely caused by the OOM (now resolved) or by the Rust optimizer issue (fixed by `#[inline(never)]`)\n6. Revert the cherry-pick before proceeding\n\nThis step de-risks the CUDA kernel code before investing in the Rust orchestration.\n\n### Change 1: Store logup Round 0 rules in proving key at keygen time\n\n**Files:**\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/pkey.rs`\n\nAdd a `LogupRound0Rules` struct to `AirDataGpu` containing the encoded logup DAG rules + interaction-to-rule-index mapping + per-challenge weight vectors. This is the same keygen-time pre-computation from commit `5bec2ee1` (reverted `52873be4`), but the stored rules are used **ONLY by the batch path** (Change 3), NOT by Phase 2. Phase 2 continues to reconstruct rules per-AIR as before, preserving the beneficial GPU pacing.\n\nThe `logup_round0_buffer_size` field is already present in `AirDataGpu` (added by the `prealloc-round0-threshold-buffers` task). This change adds the full rules alongside it.\n\n**Why this doesn't regress like commit 5bec2ee1:** That commit modified Phase 2's `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` to use keygen-time rules, removing the per-AIR CPU work that served as GPU pacing. This change does NOT modify Phase 2 at all — Phase 2 is unchanged. The keygen rules are only consumed by the new `batch_round0_small_airs` function (Change 3).\n\n### Change 2: CUDA Batched Kernels\n\n**Files:**\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu`\n\nReplicate the descriptor-array kernel design from reverted commit `0d1bb2f9`:\n\n- **`batched_zerocheck_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`**: Block-to-AIR mapping via `Round0BlockCtx` array. Each `Round0ZcCtx` descriptor has all per-AIR parameters. Only `GLOBAL=true, NEEDS_SHMEM=false` instantiation (PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS active). Uses the existing single-AIR kernel body, wrapped with a descriptor lookup preamble.\n\n- **`batched_logup_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`**: Same pattern with `Round0LogupCtx` descriptors. Uses keygen-time pre-computed rules (device pointers from Change 1).\n\n- **`batched_final_reduce_block_sums`**: Reuse the existing reduction pattern from `batch_mle.cu`.\n\n**Null intermediates:** For AIRs with `zc_intermed_cap == 0` (no zerocheck constraints) or `logup_intermed_cap == 0`, the descriptor's intermediates pointer is set to a valid offset in the arena (pointing past all used data). The kernel code already guards against `buffer_size == 0` by skipping the intermediates write path. The previous `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` (from commit `0c699f47`) occurred because GLOBAL-mode kernels unconditionally dereferenced intermediates for all blocks — the batched kernel must check `buffer_size > 0` before writing to intermediates, or the null descriptor must be excluded from the launch.\n\n### Change 3: FFI Bindings\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd `#[repr(C)]` Rust structs mirroring the CUDA descriptors:\n- `Round0BlockCtx { air_idx: u32, local_block_idx_x: u32 }`\n- `Round0ZcCtx` with fields: main_trace_ptr, preprocessed_ptr, buffer_size, num_x, skip_domain, num_cosets, eq_xis_ptr, sels_ptr, output_ptr, output_offset\n- `Round0LogupCtx` with fields: trace ptrs, encoded_rules_ptr, rules_len, weight ptrs, buffer_size, eq_3b_ptr, output_ptr, output_offset\n\nAdd extern \"C\" declarations and safe wrappers.\n\n### Change 4: Batch Orchestration Module\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0_batched.rs` (new file)\n\nAll public functions `#[inline(never)]` to prevent Rust optimizer interference.\n\n**`identify_batchable_airs(work_items, l_skip) -> Vec<bool>`**\n- Batchable if: (a) coset-parallel (`num_x * skip_domain < 32768`), (b) intermediates within budget (`< 16M Fp`), (c) AIR has zerocheck OR logup work\n- Returns all-false if fewer than 50 qualify (disabled at APC 0 with ~20 AIRs/seg)\n\n**`batch_round0_small_airs(work_items, batch_mask, pk, extract_tables, d_batch_ptr, ...) -> Result<()>`**\n1. Group batchable AIRs by `num_cosets_zc` (constraint_degree − 1) — AIRs with different num_cosets need separate launches.\n2. For each group:\n   a. Compute total intermediates size (sum of per-AIR sizes) and allocate shared arena `DeviceBuffer`\n   b. Build `Round0ZcCtx` descriptors: use proving key pointers (trace, preprocessed, zerocheck config) and arena offsets for intermediates. Output offsets point into `d_batch_array` at each AIR's `trace_idx`.\n   c. Build `Round0BlockCtx` mapping array\n   d. Upload descriptors + block_ctxs to GPU\n   e. Launch `batched_zerocheck_r0_coset_parallel_kernel` + reduction\n   f. Build `Round0LogupCtx` descriptors: use keygen-time logup rules from `pk.per_air[air_idx].other_data.logup_round0_rules` (Change 1). Compute per-challenge interaction weights from current `beta_pows` and `eq_3bs`.\n   g. Launch `batched_logup_r0_coset_parallel_kernel` + reduction\n   h. For each batched AIR: call `_round0_extract_zerocheck_poly()` and `_round0_extract_logup_polys()` to write final polynomial coefficients into `d_batch_array`\n3. Sync default stream\n\n**Per-AIR extraction (step 2h):** The ~500 extraction launches (250 AIRs × 2) add ~25ms on the default stream. This is the largest cost in the batch path. A future iteration could batch extraction too, but per-AIR extraction is simpler, uses the proven existing kernels, and keeps this change more manageable.\n\n**Per-AIR interaction weight computation (step 2f):** Computing interaction weights requires current `beta_pows` and `eq_3bs` values (challenge-dependent, can't be pre-computed at keygen). This is ~10μs per AIR of CPU arithmetic. For 250 AIRs: 2.5ms. Not a significant cost.\n\n### Change 5: Integration\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nInsert the batch path between Phase 1 and the `num_threads` branching point (line 1208). The batch path runs only in the multi-threaded case (`num_threads > 1`, i.e., APC 100/300 with ≥100 AIRs). In the single-threaded case (`num_threads <= 1`, APC 0), the batch path is skipped because `identify_batchable_airs` returns all-false (< 50 qualifying AIRs).\n\n```rust\n// After Phase 1 (work items + extract tables built, d_batch_array allocated, sync done)\nlet batch_mask = round0_batched::identify_batchable_airs(&work_items, l_skip);\nlet has_batched = batch_mask.iter().any(|&b| b);\nif has_batched {\n    round0_batched::batch_round0_small_airs(\n        &work_items, &batch_mask, self.pk, &extract_tables,\n        d_batch_array.as_mut_ptr(), l_skip,\n        &self.eq_3b_per_trace, &self.beta_pows,\n    )?;\n    // batch writes to d_batch_array on default stream; sync before worker threads start\n    current_stream_sync()?;\n}\n```\n\nIn Phase 2, the worker threads skip batched AIRs:\n```rust\nfor &item_idx in &thread_items[thread_id] {\n    if batch_mask[item_idx] { continue; }\n    process_air_round0(&work_items[item_idx], &mut bufs, batch_ptr_val as *mut EF, &extract_tables)?;\n}\n```\n\n### Change 6: Visibility\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nWiden `Round0AirWorkItem` and `Round0ExtractTables` to `pub(super)` or place `round0_batched.rs` as a submodule of `logup_zerocheck` so it has access.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: Batched eval produces the same coset-parallel NTT + constraint evaluation as per-AIR eval. Extraction uses the identical per-AIR kernels, writing to the same `d_batch_array` offsets.\n\n2. **APC 0 guard**: `MIN_BATCHABLE_AIRS = 50` disables batching at APC 0 (≤20 AIRs/seg). The single-threaded path (`num_threads <= 1`) is completely unchanged.\n\n3. **Phase 2 pacing preserved**: Phase 2's `process_air_round0` is unchanged for non-batched AIRs. The per-AIR `SymbolicConstraints::from()` + rule building remains as GPU pacing for large AIRs. Phase 2 does NOT use the keygen-time logup rules.\n\n4. **Stream ordering**: Batch path on default stream → `current_stream_sync()` → Phase 2 on per-thread streams. Batch results visible to all worker threads. Phase 2 results joined at scope exit.\n\n5. **Memory**: Shared intermediates arena sized by summing per-AIR needs. Typical size: 10-50MB for ~250 small AIRs (well within L2 cache). Per-AIR budget cap (16M Fp ≈ 64MB) prevents outliers from inflating the arena.\n\n6. **Null pointer safety**: Descriptors for AIRs with `buffer_size == 0` (no intermediates needed) get the arena base pointer as intermediates. The kernel checks `buffer_size > 0` before writing to intermediates. This avoids the `cudaErrorIllegalAddress` from the original attempt.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. **Step 0**: Run `compute-sanitizer --tool memcheck` on cherry-picked `0d1bb2f9` to diagnose the previous GPU hang (see Change 0)\n2. Build: `cd /home/georg/powdr && cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"`\n3. APC 0 regression: verify Round 0 ≤ 180ms, STARK excl trace ≤ 1810ms\n4. APC 300: expect Round 0 120-145ms (from 179ms), STARK excl trace 1046-1071ms\n5. APC 100 for completeness\n6. If APC 300 passes but Round 0 savings < 25ms: profile batch path overhead with tracing spans to identify whether descriptor building, extraction, or eval dominates\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- **Hard revert**: APC 0 STARK excl trace regresses >20ms vs 1784ms\n- **Hard revert**: Verification failure at any APC config\n- **Hard revert**: GPU OOM, `cudaErrorIllegalAddress`, or GPU hang at any APC config\n- **Soft revert**: APC 300 Round 0 improvement <10ms\n- **Soft revert**: APC 300 STARK excl trace improvement <20ms\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9086,"cache_read_input_tokens":193011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9086},"output_tokens":69,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f245fed0-0457-4d6a-9da2-744553774a6f","uuid":"41bad6fe-bc42-4363-8d11-3021eb4f9a27"}
