{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KECyftkaigEh1MjK4bZFoG","type":"tool_result","content":"86\t\n87\t### Change 4: Batch Orchestration Module\n88\t\n89\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0_batched.rs` (new file)\n90\t\n91\tAll public functions `#[inline(never)]`.\n92\t\n93\t**`identify_batchable_airs(work_items, l_skip) -> Vec<bool>`**\n94\t- Batchable if: (a) coset-parallel (`num_x * skip_domain < 32768`), (b) intermediates within budget (`< 16M Fp` per AIR), (c) AIR has zerocheck constraints or logup interactions\n95\t- Returns all-false if fewer than `MIN_BATCHABLE_AIRS = 50` qualify\n96\t- **Also** computes and logs the aggregate intermediates size for the reviewer's L2 cache concern: `info!(\"Batch Round 0: {} AIRs, aggregate intermediates: {} MB\", count, total_bytes / 1_000_000)`\n97\t\n98\t**`batch_round0_eval_only(work_items, batch_mask, pk, d_batch_ptr, ...) -> Result<BatchEvalState>`**\n99\tThis function runs on the default stream and performs **only the eval kernels**, NOT extraction. Returns a `BatchEvalState` containing per-AIR result pointers and metadata needed for extraction.\n100\t\n101\tSteps:\n102\t1. Group batchable AIRs by `num_cosets_zc` (constraint_degree − 1)\n103\t2. For each group:\n104\t   a. Compute total intermediates size (sum of per-AIR `zc_intermed_cap + logup_intermed_cap`)\n105\t   b. Allocate shared intermediates arena `DeviceBuffer`\n106\t   c. Build `Round0ZcCtx` descriptors with arena offsets\n107\t   d. Build `Round0BlockCtx` mapping\n108\t   e. Upload descriptors to GPU\n109\t   f. Launch `batched_zerocheck_r0_coset_parallel_kernel` + reduction\n110\t   g. Compute proving-time interaction weights per AIR (CPU: ~10μs/AIR using `beta_pows` and `eq_3bs`)\n111\t   h. Upload `d_numer_weights`, `d_denom_weights` to GPU\n112\t   i. Build `Round0LogupCtx` descriptors using keygen-time rules from `pk.per_air[air_idx].other_data.logup_round0_rules`\n113\t   j. Launch `batched_logup_r0_coset_parallel_kernel` + reduction\n114\t3. Return `BatchEvalState` with per-AIR eval result offsets into the intermediates arena\n115\t\n116\t**Key design: extraction is NOT done here.** Extraction is deferred to Phase 2 worker threads (Change 5).\n117\t\n118\t### Change 5: Integration\n119\t\n120\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n121\t\n122\tInsert the batch eval between Phase 1 (after `current_stream_sync()` at line 1202) and the `num_threads` branch:\n123\t\n124\t```rust\n125\t// Batch eval for small AIRs (default stream)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RS5ZqxNfusht1ytueLXki3","session_id":"f245fed0-0457-4d6a-9da2-744553774a6f","uuid":"ec65bba1-b78b-470c-bd2f-880f12a7dc26","timestamp":"2026-04-15T17:03:51.530Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aaec4e6530b710476","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RS5ZqxNfusht1ytueLXki3","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"Review optimization plan (round 3)","usage":{"total_tokens":69177,"tool_uses":44,"duration_ms":233038},"uuid":"d0b61e01-2e94-4450-b845-0152188a457e","session_id":"f245fed0-0457-4d6a-9da2-744553774a6f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RS5ZqxNfusht1ytueLXki3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have now collected all the data needed for a thorough review. Here is my assessment.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Finding 1 (Medium severity): `BatchEvalState` must own the output `DeviceBuffer`s, not just offsets into the intermediates arena\n\nThe plan says `BatchEvalState` contains \"per-AIR eval result offsets into the intermediates arena\" (Change 4, step 3). But extraction does not read from the intermediates arena. Looking at the reverted implementation (`round0_batched.rs` from commit `0d1bb2f9`, lines 396-418), extraction reads from the **output** `DeviceBuffer<EF>` (for zerocheck) and `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` (for logup), which are separate from intermediates. These are the post-reduction output buffers, not the raw intermediates.\n\nIf `BatchEvalState` only stores offsets into the intermediates arena, `extract_batched_air` has no pointer to the actual eval output. The `BatchEvalState` must own (or hold references to) the output `DeviceBuffer`s from each group's eval+reduce step, along with per-AIR stride info so extraction can index into them. The plan conflates intermediates (scratch space used during eval, freed after) with outputs (post-reduction results consumed by extraction).\n\nThis is not a design-level blocker because the fix is straightforward -- have `BatchEvalState` hold the output `DeviceBuffer`s instead of (or in addition to) intermediates offsets. But as written, an implementer would reach for the wrong pointer.\n\n**What must change:** Clarify that `BatchEvalState` owns the per-group `DeviceBuffer<EF>` (zerocheck output) and `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` (logup output), plus per-AIR stride and local index within each group, matching what extraction actually reads.\n\n### Finding 2 (Medium severity): `BatchEvalState` output buffers are allocated on the main thread's stream; extraction launches on worker thread streams -- need stream synchronization for the output buffers\n\nThe batch eval allocates output `DeviceBuffer`s on the main thread's `cudaStreamPerThread`. The `current_stream_sync()` in Change 5 ensures the batch eval kernels complete on the main thread's stream. After that, Phase 2 worker threads call `extract_batched_air`, which launches extraction kernels on each worker's own `cudaStreamPerThread`, reading from those output buffers.\n\nThe `current_stream_sync()` call on the main thread is a CPU-side sync: it blocks the main thread until the main thread's stream is idle. After that returns, any thread can safely read the output data from the GPU because the data is globally visible (stream sync implies all prior writes are committed to device memory). So the synchronization is actually correct for data visibility.\n\nHowever, there is a subtlety with `cudaFreeAsync`. The output `DeviceBuffer`s in `BatchEvalState` will be dropped when `BatchEvalState` is dropped. If `BatchEvalState` is dropped on the main thread after Phase 2 completes, the `cudaFreeAsync` call would be enqueued on the main thread's stream. But Phase 2 worker threads might still have outstanding extraction kernel launches on their streams that read from these buffers. The current code handles this because Phase 2 threads sync their streams before exiting (line 1401: `current_stream_sync()`), and the main thread joins all Phase 2 threads before continuing (line 1409-1411). So the buffer free happens after all readers are done.\n\nThis is correct **provided** `BatchEvalState` is not dropped before the Phase 2 thread join. The plan's code in Change 5 creates `batch_eval_state` before the `std::thread::scope` block, which means it lives until after the scope exits and all threads are joined. This is fine.\n\nNo change required, but the plan would benefit from a note stating that `BatchEvalState` must not be dropped until after all Phase 2 worker threads have synced their streams.\n\n### Finding 3 (Low-medium severity): Change 1 (`LogupRound0Rules` in proving key) references `d_rules` but the existing logup round0 path in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` builds rules from the `SymbolicConstraints` interaction list, not from a stored `EvalRules`\n\nIn `pkey.rs`, `AirDataGpu` already stores `zerocheck_round0: ConstraintOnlyRules<true>` which includes `d_rules` and `buffer_size`. But there is no analogous stored logup round0 rules struct. The existing code in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 191-238) builds the logup DAG and rules fresh each invocation from the `SymbolicConstraints` interaction list.\n\nThe plan's Change 1 proposes adding `LogupRound0Rules` containing `d_rules`, `rules_len`, `buffer_size`, and `dag_idx_to_rule_idx`. The `buffer_size` is already stored as `logup_round0_buffer_size` in `AirDataGpu`. The challenge-dependent weights are correctly excluded.\n\nHowever, the plan says `d_rules` contains the encoded DAG rules, but the rule encoding depends on the DAG topology which includes the interaction expressions. The DAG is deterministic given the same `SymbolicConstraintsDag`, so computing it once at keygen is correct. The `dag_idx_to_rule_idx` mapping is needed at proving time to compute weight vectors. This part is sound.\n\nThe concern is that `logup_round0_buffer_size` is already computed at keygen from this same DAG (it must be, since it is used in Phase 1 to size intermediates). Looking at the code in `pkey.rs` (line 35): `pub logup_round0_buffer_size: u32`. This confirms the DAG is already built at keygen time to extract the buffer size, then thrown away. The plan merely proposes retaining the encoded rules and the mapping.\n\nNo blocker. The approach is consistent with existing keygen patterns.\n\n### Finding 4 (Low severity): Savings table inconsistency -- \"Total (2 segments)\" does not match \"Total per segment\" doubled\n\nThe table shows per-segment savings of 9-22ms, and total savings of 17-44ms. `2 * 9 = 18`, not 17. `2 * 22 = 44`, which matches. The lower bound is off by 1ms. This is a trivial rounding issue and does not affect the plan's validity.\n\n### Finding 5 (Low severity): The savings table claims \"Small AIR CPU work in Phase 2: ~6ms/thread -> 0ms\" and \"Small AIR eval launches in Phase 2: ~5ms/thread -> 0ms\"\n\nThese are per-thread numbers. With 8 threads, this is 11ms/thread eliminated from the **critical path** (i.e., the slowest thread determines wall-clock time, not the sum). The claim of 11ms/thread improvement on the critical path is plausible only if small AIRs are on the critical path. Since work items are distributed round-robin by descending height, each thread gets ~31 small AIRs and ~8 large AIRs. The large AIRs dominate per-thread time (~24-40ms per the plan), so eliminating 11ms of small-AIR overhead could reduce the max-thread time by up to 11ms.\n\nBut the table then adds a separate \"CUDA overhead\" row saving 6-16ms. This is on top of the per-thread savings, attributed to fewer kernel launches reducing driver overhead. The total per-segment savings of 9-22ms looks like it accounts for overlap between the batch eval cost (+5-8ms) and the combined thread + overhead savings. This is plausible but hard to validate without profiling. The range is wide enough to absorb estimation error.\n\n### Finding 6 (Low severity): Change 1 struct `LogupRound0Rules` specifies `dag_idx_to_rule_idx: Vec<usize>` stored on host\n\nThis is a host-side mapping used only at proving time to compute weight vectors. It does not need to be on the GPU. This is correct. However, the plan does not specify where the `LogupRound0Rules` struct is stored. It should be a field of `AirDataGpu` (alongside the existing `logup_round0_buffer_size`), which is what the plan implies in Change 4 step 2.i: `pk.per_air[air_idx].other_data.logup_round0_rules`. This matches the existing pattern. No issue.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### 1. Simplify by running extraction on the default stream (same as reverted implementation)\n\nThe reverted commit `0d1bb2f9` ran extraction on the default stream immediately after each batch eval group. This was identified as a problem in round 2 review because it serializes extraction. But the extraction kernels are tiny (single thread block, ~64 threads, mat-vec multiply on at most ~64x64 data for small AIRs). Running 250 of these serially on the default stream takes approximately 250 * 0.01ms = 2.5ms. The plan's approach of distributing extraction to 8 worker threads saves at most 2.5ms * (7/8) = ~2.2ms over serial execution.\n\nThe complexity cost of the `BatchEvalState` struct, the extraction-on-worker-threads integration, and the extra synchronization reasoning is substantial. If the implementer finds the worker-thread approach adds significant implementation complexity, running extraction on the default stream (as the reverted code did) would be a reasonable simplification with a small (~2ms) wall-clock cost.\n\n### 2. Skip Change 0 (GPU hang diagnosis) if time-constrained\n\nThe plan's Change 0 proposes reproducing the GPU hang from the reverted commit. Since the OOM that likely caused it has been resolved (VPMM 16 MiB pages + PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS), and the plan already includes the null-pointer guard that fixes the `cudaErrorIllegalAddress`, the diagnostic step is de-risked. If implementation time is limited, Change 0 could be deferred to a post-merge investigation.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere is **no remaining design-level blocker**. Finding 1 is an ambiguity in what `BatchEvalState` contains (intermediates offsets vs. output buffer ownership), but the fix is editorial, not architectural. The stream ordering analysis in Finding 2 is correct: the `current_stream_sync()` on the main thread followed by scoped threads with per-thread stream syncs at exit is sound.\n\nThe plan is **prototype-ready** with the clarification from Finding 1: `BatchEvalState` must own the per-group output `DeviceBuffer`s (not intermediates offsets) and provide per-AIR stride/index for extraction. An implementer reading the plan as-is would likely discover this during coding, but it should be made explicit to meet the \"implementable without inventing missing design\" bar.\n\nThe strongest part of the plan is the revised extraction-on-worker-threads design (Change 5). It correctly preserves the current 8-way concurrent extraction model, uses the existing `current_stream_sync()` barrier to ensure data visibility, and fits cleanly into the existing Phase 2 `std::thread::scope` structure. The code sketch in Change 5 is close to implementation-ready.\n\nThe savings estimate of 15-35ms (1.4-3.2% of STARK excl trace) is conservative and realistic given the measured 179ms Round 0 and the plan's mechanism of eliminating ~500 kernel launches per segment. The wide range appropriately reflects uncertainty about systemic CUDA driver overhead reduction."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aaec4e6530b710476 (use SendMessage with to: 'aaec4e6530b710476' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 70588\ntool_uses: 44\nduration_ms: 233040</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f245fed0-0457-4d6a-9da2-744553774a6f","uuid":"da002e0c-bfa1-4791-ad29-3d516a53f489","timestamp":"2026-04-15T17:05:04.385Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a plan reviewer. Review the REVISED optimization plan following the review guideline.\n\nRead these files:\n1. /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/review_guideline.md — the review standard\n2. /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md — the task description\n3. /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-1800-reenable-batch-round0-small-airs/plan.md — the revised plan (THIRD version)\n\nThis is the THIRD review round. Previous reviews identified:\n- Round 1: logup rule overhead, GPU hang diagnosis, inconsistent numbers, incomplete spec\n- Round 2: extraction serialization on default stream would negate savings\n\nBoth issues were addressed in this revision:\n- Extraction now runs on Phase 2 worker threads (not default stream), preserving concurrent extraction\n- Savings estimate revised to 15-35ms (more conservative)\n- Detailed per-component savings breakdown table added\n- LogupRound0Rules struct clarified to contain only challenge-independent data\n- L2 cache verification added as runtime logging\n\nFocus your review on:\n1. Is the revised extraction-on-worker-threads design correct (stream ordering, data visibility)?\n2. Is the per-component savings table internally consistent and realistic?\n3. Are there any remaining design-level blockers?\n\nReview this plan following the review guideline. Return your response in the format: Findings, Improvements, Assessment.","agentId":"aaec4e6530b710476","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have now collected all the data needed for a thorough review. Here is my assessment.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Finding 1 (Medium severity): `BatchEvalState` must own the output `DeviceBuffer`s, not just offsets into the intermediates arena\n\nThe plan says `BatchEvalState` contains \"per-AIR eval result offsets into the intermediates arena\" (Change 4, step 3). But extraction does not read from the intermediates arena. Looking at the reverted implementation (`round0_batched.rs` from commit `0d1bb2f9`, lines 396-418), extraction reads from the **output** `DeviceBuffer<EF>` (for zerocheck) and `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` (for logup), which are separate from intermediates. These are the post-reduction output buffers, not the raw intermediates.\n\nIf `BatchEvalState` only stores offsets into the intermediates arena, `extract_batched_air` has no pointer to the actual eval output. The `BatchEvalState` must own (or hold references to) the output `DeviceBuffer`s from each group's eval+reduce step, along with per-AIR stride info so extraction can index into them. The plan conflates intermediates (scratch space used during eval, freed after) with outputs (post-reduction results consumed by extraction).\n\nThis is not a design-level blocker because the fix is straightforward -- have `BatchEvalState` hold the output `DeviceBuffer`s instead of (or in addition to) intermediates offsets. But as written, an implementer would reach for the wrong pointer.\n\n**What must change:** Clarify that `BatchEvalState` owns the per-group `DeviceBuffer<EF>` (zerocheck output) and `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>` (logup output), plus per-AIR stride and local index within each group, matching what extraction actually reads.\n\n### Finding 2 (Medium severity): `BatchEvalState` output buffers are allocated on the main thread's stream; extraction launches on worker thread streams -- need stream synchronization for the output buffers\n\nThe batch eval allocates output `DeviceBuffer`s on the main thread's `cudaStreamPerThread`. The `current_stream_sync()` in Change 5 ensures the batch eval kernels complete on the main thread's stream. After that, Phase 2 worker threads call `extract_batched_air`, which launches extraction kernels on each worker's own `cudaStreamPerThread`, reading from those output buffers.\n\nThe `current_stream_sync()` call on the main thread is a CPU-side sync: it blocks the main thread until the main thread's stream is idle. After that returns, any thread can safely read the output data from the GPU because the data is globally visible (stream sync implies all prior writes are committed to device memory). So the synchronization is actually correct for data visibility.\n\nHowever, there is a subtlety with `cudaFreeAsync`. The output `DeviceBuffer`s in `BatchEvalState` will be dropped when `BatchEvalState` is dropped. If `BatchEvalState` is dropped on the main thread after Phase 2 completes, the `cudaFreeAsync` call would be enqueued on the main thread's stream. But Phase 2 worker threads might still have outstanding extraction kernel launches on their streams that read from these buffers. The current code handles this because Phase 2 threads sync their streams before exiting (line 1401: `current_stream_sync()`), and the main thread joins all Phase 2 threads before continuing (line 1409-1411). So the buffer free happens after all readers are done.\n\nThis is correct **provided** `BatchEvalState` is not dropped before the Phase 2 thread join. The plan's code in Change 5 creates `batch_eval_state` before the `std::thread::scope` block, which means it lives until after the scope exits and all threads are joined. This is fine.\n\nNo change required, but the plan would benefit from a note stating that `BatchEvalState` must not be dropped until after all Phase 2 worker threads have synced their streams.\n\n### Finding 3 (Low-medium severity): Change 1 (`LogupRound0Rules` in proving key) references `d_rules` but the existing logup round0 path in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` builds rules from the `SymbolicConstraints` interaction list, not from a stored `EvalRules`\n\nIn `pkey.rs`, `AirDataGpu` already stores `zerocheck_round0: ConstraintOnlyRules<true>` which includes `d_rules` and `buffer_size`. But there is no analogous stored logup round0 rules struct. The existing code in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 191-238) builds the logup DAG and rules fresh each invocation from the `SymbolicConstraints` interaction list.\n\nThe plan's Change 1 proposes adding `LogupRound0Rules` containing `d_rules`, `rules_len`, `buffer_size`, and `dag_idx_to_rule_idx`. The `buffer_size` is already stored as `logup_round0_buffer_size` in `AirDataGpu`. The challenge-dependent weights are correctly excluded.\n\nHowever, the plan says `d_rules` contains the encoded DAG rules, but the rule encoding depends on the DAG topology which includes the interaction expressions. The DAG is deterministic given the same `SymbolicConstraintsDag`, so computing it once at keygen is correct. The `dag_idx_to_rule_idx` mapping is needed at proving time to compute weight vectors. This part is sound.\n\nThe concern is that `logup_round0_buffer_size` is already computed at keygen from this same DAG (it must be, since it is used in Phase 1 to size intermediates). Looking at the code in `pkey.rs` (line 35): `pub logup_round0_buffer_size: u32`. This confirms the DAG is already built at keygen time to extract the buffer size, then thrown away. The plan merely proposes retaining the encoded rules and the mapping.\n\nNo blocker. The approach is consistent with existing keygen patterns.\n\n### Finding 4 (Low severity): Savings table inconsistency -- \"Total (2 segments)\" does not match \"Total per segment\" doubled\n\nThe table shows per-segment savings of 9-22ms, and total savings of 17-44ms. `2 * 9 = 18`, not 17. `2 * 22 = 44`, which matches. The lower bound is off by 1ms. This is a trivial rounding issue and does not affect the plan's validity.\n\n### Finding 5 (Low severity): The savings table claims \"Small AIR CPU work in Phase 2: ~6ms/thread -> 0ms\" and \"Small AIR eval launches in Phase 2: ~5ms/thread -> 0ms\"\n\nThese are per-thread numbers. With 8 threads, this is 11ms/thread eliminated from the **critical path** (i.e., the slowest thread determines wall-clock time, not the sum). The claim of 11ms/thread improvement on the critical path is plausible only if small AIRs are on the critical path. Since work items are distributed round-robin by descending height, each thread gets ~31 small AIRs and ~8 large AIRs. The large AIRs dominate per-thread time (~24-40ms per the plan), so eliminating 11ms of small-AIR overhead could reduce the max-thread time by up to 11ms.\n\nBut the table then adds a separate \"CUDA overhead\" row saving 6-16ms. This is on top of the per-thread savings, attributed to fewer kernel launches reducing driver overhead. The total per-segment savings of 9-22ms looks like it accounts for overlap between the batch eval cost (+5-8ms) and the combined thread + overhead savings. This is plausible but hard to validate without profiling. The range is wide enough to absorb estimation error.\n\n### Finding 6 (Low severity): Change 1 struct `LogupRound0Rules` specifies `dag_idx_to_rule_idx: Vec<usize>` stored on host\n\nThis is a host-side mapping used only at proving time to compute weight vectors. It does not need to be on the GPU. This is correct. However, the plan does not specify where the `LogupRound0Rules` struct is stored. It should be a field of `AirDataGpu` (alongside the existing `logup_round0_buffer_size`), which is what the plan implies in Change 4 step 2.i: `pk.per_air[air_idx].other_data.logup_round0_rules`. This matches the existing pattern. No issue.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### 1. Simplify by running extraction on the default stream (same as reverted implementation)\n\nThe reverted commit `0d1bb2f9` ran extraction on the default stream immediately after each batch eval group. This was identified as a problem in round 2 review because it serializes extraction. But the extraction kernels are tiny (single thread block, ~64 threads, mat-vec multiply on at most ~64x64 data for small AIRs). Running 250 of these serially on the default stream takes approximately 250 * 0.01ms = 2.5ms. The plan's approach of distributing extraction to 8 worker threads saves at most 2.5ms * (7/8) = ~2.2ms over serial execution.\n\nThe complexity cost of the `BatchEvalState` struct, the extraction-on-worker-threads integration, and the extra synchronization reasoning is substantial. If the implementer finds the worker-thread approach adds significant implementation complexity, running extraction on the default stream (as the reverted code did) would be a reasonable simplification with a small (~2ms) wall-clock cost.\n\n### 2. Skip Change 0 (GPU hang diagnosis) if time-constrained\n\nThe plan's Change 0 proposes reproducing the GPU hang from the reverted commit. Since the OOM that likely caused it has been resolved (VPMM 16 MiB pages + PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS), and the plan already includes the null-pointer guard that fixes the `cudaErrorIllegalAddress`, the diagnostic step is de-risked. If implementation time is limited, Change 0 could be deferred to a post-merge investigation.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere is **no remaining design-level blocker**. Finding 1 is an ambiguity in what `BatchEvalState` contains (intermediates offsets vs. output buffer ownership), but the fix is editorial, not architectural. The stream ordering analysis in Finding 2 is correct: the `current_stream_sync()` on the main thread followed by scoped threads with per-thread stream syncs at exit is sound.\n\nThe plan is **prototype-ready** with the clarification from Finding 1: `BatchEvalState` must own the per-group output `DeviceBuffer`s (not intermediates offsets) and provide per-AIR stride/index for extraction. An implementer reading the plan as-is would likely discover this during coding, but it should be made explicit to meet the \"implementable without inventing missing design\" bar.\n\nThe strongest part of the plan is the revised extraction-on-worker-threads design (Change 5). It correctly preserves the current 8-way concurrent extraction model, uses the existing `current_stream_sync()` barrier to ensure data visibility, and fits cleanly into the existing Phase 2 `std::thread::scope` structure. The code sketch in Change 5 is close to implementation-ready.\n\nThe savings estimate of 15-35ms (1.4-3.2% of STARK excl trace) is conservative and realistic given the measured 179ms Round 0 and the plan's mechanism of eliminating ~500 kernel launches per segment. The wide range appropriately reflects uncertainty about systemic CUDA driver overhead reduction."}],"totalDurationMs":233040,"totalTokens":70588,"totalToolUseCount":44,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1189,"cache_read_input_tokens":66604,"output_tokens":2794,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1189},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2794,"cache_read_input_tokens":66604,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1189,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":16,"searchCount":12,"bashCount":16,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
